  "salt": "_LgVlcC3gLi8yKqdsbHVaA==",
  "text": "Satire was an Irish ritual speech. In modern usage satire is a literary work that uses wit and irony to expose vice and folly. In ancient Ireland satire had a more urgent and moral purpose, and it was poetic in form. The poet's satire changed society. The bard used the power of the king, who made generous hospitality and kept sacred vows to the goddess of sovereignty. If the king failed, blight and famine could follow, so the poet made a satire strong enough to raise boils on the king's face. A blemished king could not rule, and the failed leader was forced from power. In the law satire counted as an assault. It could not be used recklessly. An honor price was demanded for an unjust satire. There was a legal rule that satire must rest on truth. A satire had to target the living, and even a true satire required payment, though a praise-poem balanced out the damage it caused.",
